{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:growth:premortem","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:d7266593dd507ca67ffec956b641414857c6e52afdc4e83b83e77c9a0141b2ff","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.3},"questionHash":"sha256:cfe468fa9439bedeee3804a35583667745f13831322e2c91a89c02a1b211413b","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq5","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"CONJUNCT A (ICE BofA MOVE averages >= 105, 2027-2036), marginal 0.62 — far above Wave 1 (0.40 / 0.45 / 0.32). ALL THREE COMMITTED THE SAME UNIT ERROR AND TWO OF THEM NAMED IT WITHOUT PRICING IT. MOVE is a NORMALIZED BASIS-POINT yield-volatility index: for a given proportional rate vol it scales with the YIELD LEVEL. The decade averages they anchored on (~90 since 1988 per informal analyst sources, ~75-85 in 2013-2022, ~75 in 2010-19) were generated in a 1.5-2.5% nominal 10y world. The locked starting point is a 4-5% nominal 10y with a 2.60% real yield. The same proportional vol prints roughly 1.7-2.0x higher. Panelist 2 wrote \"MOVE is normalized bp vol, so with G7 10y nominals at 4-5% its floor is structurally above the ZIRP era\" and then set 0.45; panelist 3 wrote the identical sentence and set 0.32, explicitly on the grounds that \">= 105 is above every decade average on record except 1987-94 and 2022-24\" — a comparison across incommensurable rate regimes. Panelist 2's own two-state arithmetic is the cleanest refutation available: he needs stress to occupy 50% of the decade only because he set the CALM state at 75. Re-level the calm state to 95-100 for a 4-5% nominal world and the required stress share collapses to ~0.15-0.25. Against: the structural vol-selling bid that compressed MOVE through 2025-26 (the 70.9 print on 17 Jul 2026 is real and is the strongest evidence for Wave 1), and mechanical collapse if inflation settles. 0.62.\n\nCONJUNCT B (at least one MSCI World -25% peak-to-trough with G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominals failing to fall >= 50 bp, or rising >= 25 bp, in the drawdown window). B1 = 0.80: hazard-based ~78% over ten years, lifted for the AI complex at ~35-40% of MSCI World cap against ~$1.4T circular commitments backed by only ~$300B equity and ~$650B debt (a 50% derating of a 38% weight is -19% before contagion), trimmed back because the observed 2026 episode (an AI-momentum proxy -37% peak-to-trough while ACWI gained 1.2%) is evidence that the complex can break WITHOUT the index breaking. B2 = 0.46, above all three seats' 0.38. Two additions they missed. (i) The measure is G7 GDP-WEIGHTED, and Japan+Germany+France+Italy at ~0.365 of the weight have structurally less room to rally 50 bp from low absolute nominal levels, with the JGB in a rising regime at a 30-year high — so the weighted rally is mechanically damped even when the US rallies hard. (ii) The threshold is lenient in a way all three noted and none priced: a merely 30 bp rally RESOLVES YES, and fast drawdowns (2020: 33 days) frequently produce sub-50 bp moves inside the window because the rally completes after the trough. I accept their offsetting argument — the modal drawdown cause in my own distribution is an AI capex bust, a growth/deflation shock that is the shock most likely to produce a big rally — which is why I stop at 0.46 and not the 0.55 the plumbing plus measurement story alone would give. Multiple-episode adjustment on a 10-year window: B = 0.80 x 0.46 x 1.02 = 0.375.\n\nCORRELATION: strongly positive, rho ~ +0.50. A correlation-failure event is itself a MOVE spike and lifts the decade mean, and one fiscal-dominance/levered-plumbing world generates both. P(A|B) = 0.80 vs 0.62 unconditional.\n\nJOINT: 0.375 x 0.80 = 0.30 -> 30. This is a conjunction and I am still 10-14 points above the Wave-1 centre; the gap is entirely the MOVE re-leveling, which is an index-definition fact, not optimism about disorder.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v